      "dom_id": "question-59828",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "If $10$ children ate $15$ cakes, what number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of eaten cakes to children is $3:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there are $3$ eaten cakes for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ children.",
      "worked_solution": "Since $10$ children can eat $15$ cakes, the ratio of cakes to children is $15:10.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,15 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,10 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,5 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,5 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,3 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThat means that $3:2$ is equivalent to $15:10.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of eaten cakes to children is $3:2\\,$ because there are $3$ cakes for every $2\\,$ children.\"\n\nThe missing number is $2.$",
